+# Daily Grind Rewards
+
+The homepage includes a native POST sign-up form using the browser Email Verification API. No OTP or magic-link email is sent. Only a provider-signed, server-verified email proof can create an account. Unsupported browsers/providers and declined, missing, invalid, or expired proofs receive a retry explanation without account creation.
+
+## Deployment
+
+- Install with `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`. Requires Node 24+ (uses built-in SQLite).
+- Set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the exact public HTTPS origin, with no path. Set `NODE_ENV=production`.
+- Register your deployed origin for the [Chrome Email Verification origin trial](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/identity/email-verification/verifier), then set `ORIGIN_TRIAL_TOKEN` to the real token. It is delivered as a response header on the homepage; no fabricated trial token is included.
+- Environment variables must be provided by the deployment environment. `.env.example` documents them; `.env` files are not automatically loaded.
+- The runtime command is `pnpm start`. It serves both the homepage and the verification endpoint. A static-only host cannot execute verification. No build step is required.
+- Set `PORT` and optionally `HOST` for your host. Terminate HTTPS at your proxy. Do not derive the public origin from untrusted request headers.
+- Persist `data/` or set `DATABASE_PATH` to a writable SQLite path on persistent storage. Restrict access to the database and backups: they contain customer data and salted scrypt password hashes. SQLite nonce/account storage supports a single deployment; multiple hosts need a shared transactional store.
+- Google is initially allowed as an issuer. The address domain must also delegate to that issuer through its `_email-verification` TXT record. Configure `EVP_ISSUERS` as a JSON object mapping other trusted HTTPS issuer origins to an array of permitted JWKS origins when adding providers. Metadata and key requests reject redirects, enforce response size/time limits, and cannot target arbitrary token-specified origins.
+
+The customer must use a participating browser and provider, be signed in to that email provider in the same browser profile, and allow browser email verification. The first-party origin trial must be enabled. An empty proof never becomes a verified email. This flow establishes ownership, not inbox deliverability.
+
+## Protocol and endpoints
+
+`GET /` creates a 256-bit per-form nonce with a ten-minute lifetime in server-side SQLite state, keyed to an HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax session cookie (Secure on HTTPS). Each tab has its own form ID. The page is served with `Cache-Control: no-store`.
+
+`POST /api/verify-email` accepts URL-encoded `name`, `email`, `password`, `form_id`, and `token`. It checks the request Origin, consumes the session-bound nonce atomically, validates the fields, and verifies DNS delegation, trusted issuer metadata, the issuer signature, expiry/freshness, verified-email claim, holder signature, audience, nonce, and SD-JWT digest binding. No client-side verified flag is trusted. Successful verification creates the rewards account with a salted scrypt password hash; duplicate emails are rejected case-insensitively. Passwords and proof tokens are never persisted or logged.
+
+Native submission returns an accessible HTML result; API requests using `Accept: application/json` receive `{ verified, message }`. Statuses: 201 created, 400 invalid fields, 403 invalid origin, 409 expired/replayed form or existing account, 422 unavailable/invalid email proof. All retries require a fresh homepage form.
+
+This implements account registration and verification only. Account sign-in, points tracking, account recovery, and the existing linked site pages are outside this change. Add deployment-level request rate limits before exposing registration to public traffic.
+
+## Validation
+
+`pnpm run check` performs syntax checks without executing or importing the server entrypoint. `pnpm test` checks cryptographic acceptance/rejection with locally signed fixtures, nonce consumption, account uniqueness, and form markup. Tests import only verifier/store helper modules; they do not start servers or perform browser automation. No browser or live-provider verification was performed.
